ns based on the mutations we  are seeing  now it has to be said that those are  just extrapolations they are not fact  but if you look at this particular  variant  it has characteristics and it has  mutations of all the other four variants  of concern  it behaves similarly to alpha  in some diagnostic tests it behaves  similarly it's got mutations that are  very similar to the beta and gamma  variant that confer some level of immune  escape  and it has mutations that are close to  the furan cleavage site of the virus  that enables the delta variant to be  more transmissible so it looks like on  the face of it that this variant has the  the two characteristics we've been most  concerned about which is high  transmissibility and some level of  immune escape having said that  i think it's important that you know  this variant will still be amenable to  all of our public health measures our  measures of mask wearing and ventilation  and social distancing would remain as  effective against this new variant as  they did with the others  even the vaccines are quite likely why  we don't know it is a fact it's quite  likely that the vaccines would remain  quite effective  against severe disease because that's  dependent on t cells which are not  impacted by the variance to a large  extent but there may be some level of  immune escape we might see more milder  cases in those who are vaccinated but  those who are not vaccinated we would be  most concerned about  now one of the reasons you detect var  new variants so successfully in south  africa is because you are very good at  genome sequencing uh do you think you're  victims of your own success to an extent  and that actually the variant could  already be being spread in europe and  the uk  you know if you don't look for something  you won't find it and if you do find it  you have to take the consequences of  what you find  that's not any reason why we would  compromise on our surveillance our focus  is to try and contain the new variants  if and when they arise  if countries choose to punish south  africa for that you know that's that's  what they wish to do it's not something  i'm you know i'm familiar with how they  want to control their pandemic the  reality is that when we look at a  variant like the beta variant or the  delta variant probably a good example  that within a matter of three weeks  it was detected in 53 countries  so even you know no matter what you try  to do against one or two or three  countries very soon the threat of the  possibility of introduction of a new  variant becomes widespread and it it  does so because  by the time we know about a new variant  even though we are really good at what  we do here in our country there's a unit  called crisp  uh headed by professor de oliveira that  really does this  fast and within days we know the results  but you still are in a situation where  when you know there's a new variant it's  already been spreading  for you know two three weeks so that's  the part of the challenge that we deal  with in surveillance and ultimately you  know every country is going to have to  become part and parcel of trying to  control new variants it's not something  we're going to do you know by just  locking the doors on others it's going  to have to be something we tackle  together  just briefly before i let you go do you  think in light of all that that the  invariant is already in the uk  i'd be surprised if it's not  because it's already in hong kong from a  traveler in south africa  i would expect it to be there  i don't know if it's there for sure but  that's my expectation  professor kareem thank you very much  indeed for your expertise today we  really appreciate your time  absolute pleasure thank you  just some breaking news to bring you now  devon and cornwall police  have said that a 24 year old cody  ackland has been charged with murder in  the connection with the disappearance  disappearance of 18 year old bobby ann  mccloyd in p